The `:has()` pseudo-class can be used to select a parent element that contains a specific child [1]. The example provided demonstrates how to style a `<section>` element when it contains a child with the `featured` class. Here is the relevant code snippet:

```css
section:has(.featured) {
  border: 2px solid blue;
}
```

This CSS will apply a blue border to any `<section>` that contains an element with the class `featured`.

To add state to a React component, you can use either a class component with the `state` property or the `useState` hook. Here are examples of both methods:

### Using Class Component
```js
import { Component } from 'react';

export default class Counter extends Component {
  state = {
    name: 'Taylor',
    age: 42,
  };

  handleNameChange = (e) => {
    this.setState({
      name: e.target.value
    });
  }

  handleAgeChange = () => {
    this.setState({
      age: this.state.age + 1
    });
  };

  render() {
    return (
      <>
        <input
          value={this.state.name}
          onChange={this.handleNameChange}
        />
        <button onClick={this.handleAgeChange}>
          Increment age
        </button>
        <p>Hello, {this.state.name}. You are {this.state.age}.</p>
      </>
    );
  }
}
```

### Using `useState` Hook
```js
import { useState } from 'react';

export default function Form() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('Taylor');
  const [age, setAge] = useState(42);

  return (
    <>
      <input
        value={name}
        onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)}
      />
      <button onClick={() => setAge(age + 1)}>
        Increment age
      </button>
      <p>Hello, {name}. You are {age}.</p>
    </>
  );
}
```

Both methods allow you to manage state within your component. The class component approach is used in React versions prior to 16.8, while the `useState` hook is available from React 16.8 onwards and is recommended for new projects due to its simplicity and reusability.

The Vue Composition API allows you to declare computed properties and other reactive state within a component. Here is a basic example of how to declare a computed property using the Composition API:

```vue
<script setup>
import { ref, computed } from 'vue'

// reactive state
const count = ref(0)

// computed property
const doubleCount = computed(() => count.value * 2)

// functions that mutate state and trigger updates
function increment() {
  count.value++
}

// lifecycle hooks
onMounted(() => {
  console.log(`The initial count is ${count.value}.`)
})
</script>

<template>
  <button @click="increment">Count is: {{ count }}, Double Count is: {{ doubleCount }}</button>
</template>
```

In this example, `doubleCount` is a computed property that returns `count.value * 2`. This property will automatically update whenever `count` changes, reflecting the reactive nature of the Composition API.

To create a dynamic route segment in the Next.js App Router, you define a segment in your route that captures a value from the URL. This is done by placing square brackets `[]` around the segment name. For example, in the route `app/blog/[slug]/page.js`, `[slug]` is a dynamic segment that captures the value from the URL path.

When a user navigates to a URL like `/blog/a`, the captured value `a` is passed to your page via the `params` prop as an object, in this case `{ slug: 'a' }`. You can access this value in your page component like so:

```jsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.js
export default function BlogPost({ params }) {
  return <div>Blog post for slug: {params.slug}</div>;
}
```

Dynamic segments are often referred to as path params, route params, or URL params [1].

Dynamic segments that appear before the root layout are root parameters and can be read from any Server Component with `next/root-params` [2].

The `useState` composable in Nuxt creates a reactive and SSR-friendly shared state [1][2]. It is an SSR-friendly `ref` replacement, ensuring that its value is preserved after server-side rendering and shared across all components using a unique key [2][3]. The data inside `useState` must be serializable to JSON, meaning it should not contain classes, functions, or symbols [2][3].

Here is an example of how to use the `useState` composable:

```ts
// app/composables/useFoo.ts
export const useFoo = () => {
  return useState('foo', () => 'bar')
}
```

Or using the default export:

```ts
// app/composables/use-foo.ts
export default function () {
  return useState('foo', () => 'bar')
}
```

In a component, you can then import and use `useFoo` like this:

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
const foo = useFoo()
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    {{ foo }}
  </div>
</template>
```

A union type in TypeScript is a type formed from two or more other types, representing values that may be any one of those types [2]. For example, `number | string` is a union type that can hold either a `number` or a `string` [2][3].

To work with a value of a union type, you need to narrow the union with code to determine which specific type the value is. This can be done using type guards like `typeof` or `Array.isArray` [3][4]. For instance, in the function `printId(id: number | string)`, you can narrow the union by checking the type of `id`:

```ts
function printId(id: number | string) {
  if (typeof id === "string") {
    // In this branch, id is of type 'string'
    console.log(id.toUpperCase());
  } else {
    // Here, id is of type 'number'
    console.log(id);
  }
}
```

Union types can also be used when all members have a common property. For example, both `string` and `number[]` have a `slice` method, so you can use `x.slice(0, 3)` on a union type `number[] | string` without needing to narrow the union [3].

In summary, a union type in TypeScript allows a variable to hold values of multiple types, and you can narrow the union to work with specific types using type guards [2][3][4].

A Next.js App Routerban adatok lekérése a `fetch` függvény használatával történik. Ez a függvény a szerveroldali kérések esetén saját folyamatos gyorsítótár és újrapróbálkozás beállításokat állít be.

A kód példa a `fetch` használatára a szerveroldali komponensben:

```tsx
export default async function Page() {
  let data = await fetch('https://api.vercel.app/blog');
  let posts = await data.json();
  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

Ebben a példában a `fetch` függvény aszinkronan hívódik meg, és a visszatérő adatokat a `data.json()` metódus átalakítja JSON formátumba. [1]

A React useEffect hook használatakor fontos megérteni, hogy mikor és milyen céllal használjuk. A useEffect hook azon esetekben szolgál, amikor egy komponens szinkronizálása egy külső rendszerrel szükséges. Például, ha egy időzítőt szeretnénk indítani, amely minden másodpercenként növeli egy állapotváltozat értékét, a useEffect hasznos lehet.

A useEffect függvény két paramétert vár: a `setup` függvényt és a `dependencies` tömböt. A `setup` függvényben írhatjuk az effect logikáját. Ez a függvény lehetővé teszi, hogy a komponens minden módosítás után végrehajtott műveleteket végrehajtsa. Ha a `setup` függvény visszatér egy *cleanup* függvénnyel, akkor a cleanup függvény a korábbi értékekkel lesz meghívva, majd a setup függvény a új értékekkel.

A példa a következőt mutatja be:
```js
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

export default function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const intervalId = setInterval(() => {
      setCount(c => c + 1); // ✅ Pass a state updater
    }, 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(intervalId);
  }, []); // ✅ Now count is not a dependency

  return <h1>{count}</h1>;
}
```

A példában a `setCount` függvénybe egy állapotfrissítőt (state updater) helyettesítettük az állapotváltozat értékével, így a useEffect nem függ többé a `count` változóról, és nem fog újra indítani az időzítőt minden állapotmódosítás után.

Tehát a useEffect használatához, amikor egy komponens idősemlékeket (pl. időzítőket) kezel, vagy egy külső rendszert (pl. egy API-t) szinkronizál, és ezek módosulnak, de a komponens nem függ tőlük, a useEffect hasznos lehet.

To fetch data on the server in Next.js and Nuxt, you can use different methods as described below:

### Next.js
In Next.js, you can use the `fetch` API to make server-side requests. The `fetch` function in Next.js allows you to set caching and revalidation semantics for server-side requests. Here is an example of fetching data in a Next.js component:

```tsx
export default async function Page() {
  let data = await fetch('https://api.vercel.app/blog')
  let posts = await data.json()
  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

### Nuxt
In Nuxt, you can use the `$fetch` helper for making HTTP requests. The `$fetch` helper is globally available in your Vue app or API routes. It is particularly useful for server-side rendering as it can emulate requests during server-side rendering, saving an additional API call. Here is an example of fetching data in a Nuxt component:

```ts
<script setup lang="ts">
const { data } = await useAsyncData('count', (_nuxtApp, { signal }) => $fetch('/api/count', { signal }))
</script>
```

```ts
// server/api/count.ts
export default defineEventHandler((event) => {
  return { count: 1 }
})
```

### Differences
- **Caching and Revalidation**: In Next.js, the `fetch` function allows you to set caching and revalidation semantics for server-side requests. In Nuxt, the `$fetch` helper is used for making HTTP requests, but it does not directly handle caching and revalidation in the same way as Next.js.
- **Server-Side Rendering**: Nuxt's `$fetch` can emulate requests during server-side rendering, which can save an additional API call. In Next.js, you can set caching and revalidation semantics, but it does not have the same feature of emulating requests during server-side rendering.
- **State Transfer**: When using `$fetch` in Nuxt components without wrapping it with `useAsyncData`, the fetch will be executed on both the server and the client side because the client has to get the data again. In Next.js, you can use `fetch` directly within Server Components to avoid this double fetch issue.
